  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 245 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nModel guidance continues to show signs of a small upper-level \nridge system exiting the Great Plains this weekend. Behind this \nridge, a mini-trough enters the region, which guidance also \nshows is likely to be positively tilted. Thus, 300-500 mb flow \nis likely to be meridional through Sunday. North to northeast \nflow aloft should dominate the weekend until another, much \nlarger, ridging system starts to build throughout the County \nWarning Area (CWA) on Monday. Ensembles mostly agree on a \ntransition to zonal westerly flow Monday as the ridge axis \npasses through the region. Thereafter, southwesterlies are \nexpected as the ridge propagates eastward into the Missouri and \nMississippi river valleys. At the surface, high pressure \nprevails through Monday. There is medium confidence on a frontal\npassage Tuesday, but nothing is expected to come of it other \nthan a windshift.\n\nGuidance foresees a lack of any significant weather through \nthis long-term forecast period as dry air takes control. \nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) currently stand at 5% or \nless for the entire CWA throughout the weekend and beginning of \nnext week. Dewpoints in the 30s and 40s as well as Relative \nHumidities (RHs) in the 30-50% range signal dry air not only \naloft but at the surface as well. Cooler than normal \ntemperatures present initially as highs in the 50s are expected \neverywhere in the Tri-State area through Saturday. Ensembles \nthen project a bit of a warming trend with this ridging pattern \nas highs in the 60s are more likely heading into next week. \nOvernight lows are also forecast to be colder earlier in this \nfour- day period, with around freezing to below freezing lows \nSaturday/Sunday. Slightly warmer low temperatures are expected \nMonday and Tuesday mornings as upper 30s to low 40s are \nprojected. Fire weather concerns remain very low as RH values \nmostly stay above 25% and surface winds are forecasted to be on \nthe weaker side (5-15 mph gusting up to 20 mph).",
